### Bug Description

<html>
<body>
<!--StartFragment--><html><head></head><body><h2><span>Bug Description</span></h2><p><span>The Compression node's </span><strong><span>Decompress</span></strong><span> operation appears to evaluate dynamic expressions in the "Input Binary Field(s)" parameter only against the first input item.</span></p><p><span>When processing multiple items containing different binary property names, the node may decompress an unintended file instead of the file selected by the expression.</span></p><p><span>The execution completes successfully without reporting an error.</span></p><h2><span>Steps to Reproduce</span></h2><ol start="1"><li><p><span>Create multiple input items containing gzip-compressed binary files.</span></p></li><li><p><span>Ensure the same target filename is stored under different binary property names in each item.</span></p></li><li><p><span>Add a Compression node with the operation set to </span><code><span>Decompress</span></code><span>.</span></p></li><li><p><span>Set "Input Binary Field(s)" to the following expression:</span></p></li></ol><pre><code><span>{{ Object.keys($binary).find(
  key =&gt; $binary<span>[key]</span>.fileName === 'target.json.gz'
) }}</span></code></pre><ol start="5"><li><p><span>Execute the node with all items in a single execution.</span></p></li></ol><h3><span>Example Input (Synthetic Data)</span></h3><p><span>The following example uses generic filenames and illustrates the observed behavior.</span></p>
Item | data0 | data1 | data2
-- | -- | -- | --
1 | other-a.json.gz | target.json.gz | other-b.json.gz
2 | target.json.gz | other-b.json.gz | other-a.json.gz
3 | other-a.json.gz | other-b.json.gz | target.json.gz
4 | other-b.json.gz | target.json.gz | other-a.json.gz

<h3><span>Expected Behavior</span></h3><p><span>The expression should be evaluated independently for each input item.</span></p><p><span>Expected binary property selection:</span></p><pre><code><span>Item 1: data1
Item 2: data0
Item 3: data2
Item 4: data1</span></code></pre><p><span>Expected decompressed output:</span></p><pre><code><span>Item 1: target.json
Item 2: target.json
Item 3: target.json
Item 4: target.json</span></code></pre><h3><span>Actual Behavior</span></h3><p><span>The node appears to reuse the binary property selected for the first item (</span><code><span>data1</span></code><span>) across all items.</span></p><p><span>For the synthetic example above, this would result in:</span></p><pre><code><span>Item 1: target.json
Item 2: other-b.json
Item 3: other-b.json
Item 4: target.json</span></code></pre><p><span>This matches the failure pattern observed during execution: files located under the binary property selected for the first item are decompressed, even when they do not match the requested filename.</span></p><p><span>No error is raised if the incorrectly selected binary property exists.</span></p><h2><span>Suspected Root Cause</span></h2><p><span>Source code inspection suggests that the Decompress operation uses a hardcoded item index when evaluating the binary property parameter.</span></p><p><span>File:</span></p><p><code><span>packages/nodes-base/nodes/Compression/Compression.node.ts</span></code></p><p><span>Relevant implementation:</span></p><pre><code><span>for (let i = 0; i &lt; length; i++) {
  try {
    if (operation === 'decompress') {
      const binaryPropertyNames = this.getNodeParameter(
        'binaryPropertyName', 0
      )
        .split(',')
        .map((key) =&gt; key.trim());</span></code></pre><p><span>The </span><code><span>getNodeParameter()</span></code><span> call explicitly uses index </span><code><span>0</span></code><span>, despite executing inside a loop over the input items.</span></p><p><span>Later, binary data is accessed using the current item index:</span></p><pre><code><span>const binaryData = this.helpers.assertBinaryData(
  i, binaryPropertyName
);</span></code></pre><p><span>This appears to cause the following behavior:</span></p><ol start="1"><li><p><span>The parameter expression is evaluated using the first input item.</span></p></li><li><p><span>The resulting binary property name is reused for subsequent items.</span></p></li><li><p><span>Binary data is retrieved from the current item using that property name.</span></p></li><li><p><span>If the property exists but contains another file, that file is decompressed instead.</span></p></li></ol><h3><span>Source Code Reference</span></h3><p><a href="https://github.com/n8n-io/n8n/blob/master/packages/nodes-base/nodes/Compression/Compression.node.ts"><span>https://github.com/n8n-io/n8n/blob/master/packages/nodes-base/nodes/Compression/Compression.node.ts</span></a></p><p><span>The hardcoded index was identified during source inspection of n8n 2.33.5, n8n 2.45.4, and the </span><code><span>master</span></code><span> branch.</span></p><p><span>This does not imply that every intermediate release was independently verified.</span></p><h2><span>Suggested Fix</span></h2><p><span>The Decompress operation should evaluate dynamic binary property expressions against the corresponding input item.</span></p><p><span>A possible correction would be:</span></p><pre><code><span>const binaryPropertyNames = this.getNodeParameter(
  'binaryPropertyName', i
)
  .split(',')
  .map((key) =&gt; key.trim());</span></code></pre><p><span>However, the Compress operation already uses version-dependent item index handling:</span></p><pre><code><span>let binaryPropertyNameIndex = 0;

if (nodeVersion &gt; 1) {
  binaryPropertyNameIndex = i;
}</span></code></pre><p><span>A similar approach may be appropriate for Decompress to preserve compatibility with older node versions.</span></p><p><span>The exact implementation should be evaluated by the maintainers.</span></p><h2><span>Suggested Regression Test</span></h2><p><span>A regression test could use synthetic binary items containing different compressed files.</span></p><p><span>The test should verify that:</span></p><ul><li><p><span>Dynamic binary field expressions are evaluated for each individual item.</span></p></li><li><p><span>The correct compressed file is selected regardless of binary property ordering.</span></p></li><li><p><span>No unintended file is decompressed.</span></p></li><li><p><span>Existing workflows and older node versions remain compatible.</span></p></li></ul><h2><span>Workaround</span></h2><p><span>Normalize the target binary property before passing items to the Compression node.</span></p><p><span>For example, a Code node can select the desired file by filename and assign it to a fixed binary property:</span></p><pre><code><span>return $input.all().map((item, index) =&gt; {
  const entry = Object.entries(item.binary ?? {}).find(
    (<span>[key, file]</span>) =&gt; file.fileName === 'target.json.gz'
  );

  if (!entry) {
    throw new Error(
      `Target file not found in item ${index + 1}`
    );
  }

  return {
    json: item.json,
    binary: {
      data: entry<span>[1]</span>
    },
    pairedItem: { item: index }
  };
});</span></code></pre><p><span>The Compression node can then use the fixed binary field </span><code><span>data</span></code><span>.</span></p><h2><span>AI Assistance Disclosure</span></h2><p><strong><span>AI assistance was used to investigate the issue, identify the suspected root cause, and propose a potential fix.</span></strong></p><p><span>Specifically, ChatGPT (OpenAI) assisted with:</span></p><ul><li><p><span>Analyzing the observed execution behavior.</span></p></li><li><p><span>Identifying the relationship between binary property names and incorrectly decompressed files.</span></p></li><li><p><span>Reviewing the relevant n8n source code.</span></p></li><li><p><span>Identifying the hardcoded item index as the suspected cause.</span></p></li><li><p><span>Suggesting a possible code correction and regression test.</span></p></li><li><p><span>Drafting and anonymizing this issue report.</span></p></li></ul><p><span>The reported failure pattern originates from actual n8n execution observations.</span></p><p><span>The reproduction example in this report is </span><strong><span>synthetic</span></strong><span> and has not been independently executed as an automated test.</span></p><p><span>The proposed fix has not been implemented or validated in a running n8n instance.</span></p><p><span>The maintainers should independently verify the diagnosis and evaluate compatibility requirements before applying a change.</span></p></body></html><!--EndFragment-->
</body>
</html>

### To Reproduce

1. Create multiple input items, each containing several gzip-compressed binary files.
2. Ensure that the same target file (`target.json.gz`) is stored under different binary property names (`data0`, `data1`, `data2`, etc.) across items.
3. Add a Compression node and select the **Decompress** operation.
4. Set **Input Binary Field(s)** to:
   ```javascript
   {{ Object.keys($binary).find(key => $binary[key].fileName === 'target.json.gz') }}
   ```
5. Execute the node with multiple items simultaneously.

**Actual result:** The node appears to evaluate the expression only against the first item and reuses the resulting binary property name for all subsequent items. This causes incorrect files to be decompressed when the binary property names differ.

### Expected behavior

The Compression node should evaluate the **Input Binary Field(s)** expression independently for each input item.

Each item should have its own matching binary property selected, regardless of the property's name or position.

In this example, every output item should contain the decompressed `target.json` file, and no unrelated files should be decompressed.

